                    briefing_text_r1:       `Resist the instinct to leap to a slick new offering. First map why the <strong>current</strong> print model is failing: a commoditised value proposition, a gross margin compressed from 34% to 22%, and a EUR 95k/month plant dragging the P&L. The decline is <strong>structural, not cyclical</strong> — continuing projects cash exhaustion in ~14 months. The discipline of Dauchy's framework is to diagnose before you redesign. And name the real crown jewel: not the printing press, but eight years of content and relationships with ~600 regional SMEs. <em>Redesign without diagnosis is guesswork.</em>`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Step 2 — Redesign the value proposition`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Build the new offer <strong>outside-in</strong>, from the customer's job — not from what your printing press happens to make. Your 600 SMEs don't wake up wanting design tools; they wake up panicking that their website and social feeds are stale and they can't manage them. State <em>who</em> the customer is, <em>what job</em> you are hired to do, <em>why you can win it</em> (the content backbone, the existing relationships) — and what you will deliberately <strong>not</strong> do. Then size the prize honestly: only an estimated <strong>35–45%</strong> of the base are realistic digital-subscription prospects.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Step 3 — Choose the monetization logic`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The same value proposition can be monetised in radically different ways — project fees, recurring subscription, a platform take-rate, or freemium — and each reshapes the cash-flow shape, not just the total. A subscription is more valuable long-term but <strong>pays slowly and compounds</strong>: you carry the cost of the pivot now and the revenue lands much later. The trap is modelling recurring revenue as if it were project work and missing the <em>cash trough before break-even</em>. The pivot needs ~EUR 700k upfront against your EUR 1.8M runway. Do you reach break-even before the cash runs out, or die solvent-on-paper?`,
