                    briefing_text_r4:       `A business model is viable only if its parts reinforce each other. Test whether your value proposition and revenue logic actually fit the resources and cost base — and decide what must change. A scalable digital subscription served by a project-based, plant-heavy operation is <strong>two business models fighting inside one P&L</strong> — exactly what your lead investor said they'd block. Which costs reinforce the new model, and which are stranded? Reconfigure the EUR 95k/month plant, reshape the 38-person team, and — critically — <strong>retain the two senior designers</strong> a Paris agency is courting: they are the engine of any digital build.`,
                    briefing_title_r5:      `Step 5 — Pitch the reinvented model to the board`,
                    briefing_text_r5:       `Present the complete model to the board and the lead seed investor. It must be <strong>internally consistent</strong> — value proposition ↔ monetization ↔ operations describing <em>one</em> company, not three — create a credible path off the declining print P&L, and survive the cash constraint. A strong model is coherent, not a collection of clever ideas. Close by stating the projected operating-margin trajectory and the <strong>single biggest risk</strong> you are accepting — most honestly, the conversion of the 600-client base. The investor will block a plan that just bolts digital onto a print P&L.`,

                    r1_diag_label:          `Before you redesign anything — what is actually breaking the print model?`,
