                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_structural:    `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> The decline is structural — a commoditised value proposition, an eroding margin AND a fixed-cost drag, all at once. Now you know what your new model must fix rather than inherit. You've earned the right to redesign.`,
                    coach_r1_margin:        `<strong>Cost-cutting alone is the board's exhausted answer.</strong> Squeezing the print margin buys months, not a future — the value proposition itself is commoditised and the channel is shifting. You're treating a symptom, not the structural cause.`,
                    coach_r1_sales:         `<strong>More reps selling a dying product is throwing good money after bad.</strong> The print decline is structural channel-shift, not an effort gap. Hiring salespeople to push commoditised print just burns runway faster.`,
                    coach_r1_skip:          `<strong>You've proposed a slick subscription, but you never said why print is dying.</strong> Is it the value proposition, the margin, the cost base, or all three? If you don't know what's broken, how do you know your new model fixes it rather than inheriting the same flaw? Diagnose first.`,
                    coach_r1_plant:         `<strong>The plant is a liability, not the crown jewel.</strong> It carries EUR 95k/month of fixed cost a digital model would strand. The real asset is eight years of content and relationships with 600 SMEs — build on that, not the printing press.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>Half-hearted about the redesign?</strong> If the team won't name the print decline as terminal, it will keep cost-cutting the old model and call it a strategy. The board has lost patience with incremental cuts — commit to the reinvention.`,
                    coach_r2_job:           `<strong>That's outside-in design.</strong> Your SMEs don't want design tools — they want their digital presence handled because they can't manage it themselves. Anchoring the value proposition in that job, on top of your content backbone, is exactly where you can win.`,
                    coach_r2_insideout:     `<strong>“We're great at design, so we'll sell design tools.”</strong> That's inside-out — built from your printing press, not the customer's job. Your 600 SMEs wake up panicking that their website and social feeds are stale, not wishing for a design tool. Start from their job.`,
                    coach_r2_print:         `<strong>Doubling down on premium print is honouring the legacy, not escaping it.</strong> It's a shrinking niche in a structurally declining channel. It may slow the bleed, but it doesn't reinvent the model — and the board asked for reinvention.`,
                    coach_r2_everything:    `<strong>Everything to everyone is a value proposition for no one.</strong> Print, web, social, ads and events at once spreads your thin runway across too many fronts and confuses the unfair advantage. A strong proposition is sharp, not exhaustive.`,
                    coach_r2_unfocused:     `<strong>Right job, blurry focus.</strong> You've named the customer's job well, but refusing to say what you will NOT do keeps the old project-shop sprawl. Strategy is as much about deliberate exclusion as about the offer.`,
                    coach_r2_overconvert:   `<strong>You're betting the company on a conversion number you haven't defended.</strong> Maybe 35–45% of the 600 are realistic digital prospects — the rest are too small or too analogue. What happens to the model if only ~200 convert? Size the crown jewel honestly.`,
                    coach_r3_subscription:  `<strong>A recurring subscription fits the managed-service job.</strong> It compounds, deepens the customer relationship, and turns your content backbone into an annuity — provided you model its slow-then-compounding cash shape honestly.`,
                    coach_r3_platform:      `<strong>A platform take-rate is a legitimate path — but a different operating logic, not a feature.</strong> It needs self-serve scale and network effects, which reshape your cost-to-scale entirely. Make sure your operations match a platform, not a service.`,
                    coach_r3_project:       `<strong>One-off project fees just re-runs the model that's dying.</strong> Project revenue doesn't compound and keeps you on the transactional treadmill you're trying to escape. The cash-flow shape barely changes — and neither does the structural problem.`,
                    coach_r3_freemium:      `<strong>Freemium can build a funnel, but it's the slowest cash shape of all.</strong> A free tier delays revenue even further while you carry the cost now — dangerous against a 14-month runway. Be sure the conversion math closes before the cash does.`,
                    coach_r3_frontload:     `<strong>You've modelled subscriptions like project work.</strong> You assumed the EUR 700k lands in year one — but recurring revenue pays slowly and compounds. The cost of the pivot is now; the revenue is much later. Re-run the 14-month curve, or you'll die solvent-on-paper.`,
                    coach_r3_underinvest:   `<strong>You've starved the build.</strong> Too little upfront and the platform, retraining and content tooling never reach a credible standard — you protect cash but ship a half-built model the investor won't fund.`,
                    coach_r4_reconfigure:   `<strong>That's coherence.</strong> Winding down most print and keeping only the slice that reinforces the digital model strips out the EUR 95k/month drag and aligns the cost base with the promise. The operation now serves the new model rather than fighting it.`,
                    coach_r4_keep:          `<strong>That's two business models fighting inside one P&L.</strong> A scalable digital promise carrying the full EUR 95k/month plant is exactly what your investor said they'd block. Which costs reinforce the new model, and which are simply stranded?`,
                    coach_r4_closeall:      `<strong>Closing everything overnight is incoherence in the other direction.</strong> You strand all print revenue and the cash it still throws off before the digital model can replace it — a self-inflicted cash shock that deepens the trough. Reconfigure, don't detonate.`,
                    coach_r4_teamasis:      `<strong>A project-shaped team can't deliver a subscription unchanged.</strong> Recurring service needs different roles, rhythms and incentives than one-off project work. Leaving the team as-is guarantees the operation contradicts the revenue model.`,
                    coach_r4_gutall:        `<strong>Cutting to the bone before the new model can absorb the work strands capability you'll need.</strong> Slashing headcount first looks decisive but leaves no one to build or deliver the digital offering. Right-size to the new model, don't just shrink.`,
                    coach_r4_designershope: `<strong>Hoping the designers stay is a plan built on luck.</strong> They are your only people who can build the digital offering and a Paris agency is courting them. If they walk, the whole pivot loses its engine. Actively retain them.`,
                    coach_r4_designersrelease:`<strong>Letting your two senior designers go guts the pivot.</strong> They are the engine of any digital build; cheaper juniors can't replace eight years of craft and client knowledge. You've just removed the capability your new model depends on.`,
                    coach_r4_loweffort:     `<strong>You haven't stress-tested the model for contradictions.</strong> A lovely value proposition, a clever revenue model and a lean operation can still describe three different companies. Without resolving the contradictions, the coherence the investor demands isn't there.`,
                    coach_r5_coherent:      `<strong>That's a board-ready model.</strong> Value proposition, monetization and operations reinforce each other, funded inside the runway — one coherent company, not three good ideas. This is the path off the declining print P&L the board asked for.`,
                    coach_r5_bolton:        `<strong>“This is just digital bolted onto a print P&L.”</strong> That's precisely what your lead investor said they would block — a new offering on top of an unchanged operation, with no real change in operating logic. The components don't reinforce each other, so the model isn't strong.`,
                    coach_r5_vision:        `<strong>A bold vision with big numbers isn't a business model.</strong> Ambition light on coherence is exactly the “collection of good ideas” trap — your value proposition, revenue and operations have to describe one company before the numbers mean anything.`,
                    coach_r5_riskhonest:    `<strong>Naming the conversion risk honestly is what makes you credible.</strong> You sized the crown jewel honestly and showed the plan if only ~200 of the 600 convert. A founder who names their biggest risk — and has a plan for it — earns the board's confidence.`,
                    coach_r5_risknone:      `<strong>“No real risk” is the least credible thing you can say.</strong> Every reinvention under a cash clock carries risk, and the obvious one is the conversion of the 600 clients. Claiming the plan is watertight tells the board you haven't pressure-tested it.`,
                    coach_r5_overmargin:    `<strong>That margin is not defensible.</strong> Over-claiming a high operating margin on a freshly pivoted recurring model signals you haven't modelled the cash trough or the cost of acquisition. A credible low-teens trajectory beats an incredible big number.`,

